-- title:
--   Standard-position points of Ω come from adic points
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $K$, let $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$, and let $C$ be a field with decidable equality carrying a valuation $\mathrm{v}$ with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, together with a $K$-algebra structure; let $R$ be a commutative $\mathcal{O}$-algebra mapping to $C$, all structure maps forming scalar towers over $\mathcal{O}$. Let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformiser, i.e. an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K$ satisfies $\mathrm{v}(\varpi)^N \le \mathrm{v}(a) \le \mathrm{v}(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume `IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`: $\pi$ is irreducible; $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly the valuation ring $\{\mathrm{v}(c) \le 1\}$; $R$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$; the elements of $K$ of valuation at most $1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal{O}$; the image of $K$ in $C$ is closed; and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Let $w \in C$ satisfy either $w \in$ `affinoid ϖ 0`, that is $\mathrm{v}(w) \le 1$ and $\mathrm{v}(w - a) \ge 1$ for every $a \in K$ with $\mathrm{v}(a) \le 1$, or $\mathrm{v}(\varpi) < \mathrm{v}(w) < 1$. Then there is an `AdicPoint K π R`, i.e. a system of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — for each full $\mathcal{O}$-lattice $M \subset K^2$ a submodule of the base change of $M$ with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for homotheties by $K^\times$, and nondegenerate at every prime — compatible under the transition maps, whose coordinate `toOmega C`, the unique $z \in C$ with $(z,1)$ in the $C$-span of the image of `stdLine`, equals $w$.
--
--   This is the surjectivity statement, in standard position, behind the identification of the adic points of the formal upper half plane with the rigid-analytic space $\Omega$, as in Drinfeld's construction and its exposition by Boutot and Carayol. It is the input to [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.AdicPoint.toOmega_surjOn`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.AdicPoint.toOmega_surjOn), from which surjectivity onto all of $\Omega$ is obtained by moving an arbitrary point into the vertex affinoid or the standard annulus.
